<section xml:id="sec-trigonometric-ratios-beyond-acute-angles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trigonometric Ratios Beyond Acute Angles</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurement and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.5 Trigonometry II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine trigonometric ratios of angles greater than 90 degrees in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <activity>
        <title>Magic Unit Circle</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Work in groups to explore how trigonometric ratios change for angles greater than 90 degrees using a unit circle.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each group will create a unit circle on manila paper, label quadrants, and place angle values in the correct region. Then compare the signs and values of sine, cosine and tangent as the angle moves through each quadrant.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a unit circle on manila paper and mark the four quadrants clearly with different colours.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write different angle measures on sticky notes, including acute, obtuse, and reflex angles.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Place each sticky note on the quadrant where the angle ends on the unit circle. Be ready to explain your choice.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Discuss how the signs of sin, cos and tan change in each quadrant, and how the unit circle helps determine those signs for angles beyond 90 degrees.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan -->

    <insight>
        <title>Key Takeaway</title>
        <p>
            The unit circle shows that sine and cosine for angles beyond 90 degrees depend on the quadrant, while tangent depends on both sine and cosine signs.
        </p>
        <p>
            Understanding quadrant signs helps learners determine trigonometric ratios for obtuse and reflex angles without memorising each value separately.
        </p>
        <p>
            
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the sine, cosine, and tangent of <m>120^\circ</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p> 
               Since <m>120^\circ</m> is between <m>90^\circ</m> and <m>180^\circ</m>, it lies in the second quadrant. 
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
